> On Oct 31, 2014, at 9:57 PM, Linas Mockus <linasm...@gmail.com> wrote:

...

> For example, the following R code:
> glm(trade ~ exporter + importer + distance - 1, family = "poisson")
>

Isn't the above model equal to:

  trade[i] ~ poisson(coeff1 * exporter[i]
                     + coeff2 * importer[i]
                     + coeff3 * distance[i]);

I thought you needed to write (1 | exporter) and (1 | importer)
if you wanted the equivalent of the following Stan model:

> Seemingly equivalent stan code doesn't converge:
>     for (i in 1 : N) {
>         mu [i] <- coeff * distance [i] + A [exporter [i]] + B [importer [i]];
>         trade [i] ~ poisson (exp (mu [i]));
>     }

But I may be confusing lme4 notation --- I've never understood these
R expressions very well.

And +1 to Ben's suggestion to use poisson_log and Andrew's suggestion
to have a prior on coeff, A, and B.  Even with very weak priors or no
priors, Stan should converge unless the arrangement of exporter[i] and
importer[i] lead to non-identifiability.  It just might take a very long
time if there's a very broad posterior to explore.

Unlike in BUGS, you don't need to define these mu[i].  If you do, then you

can just write:

  for (i in 1:N)
    mu[i] <- coeff * distance [i] + A[exporter[i]] + B[importer[i]];

  trade ~ poisson_log(mu);

- Bob

